# Design of a Unique Hole‐Transporting Molecule via Introducing a Chloro‐Involved Chelating Moiety for High‐Performance Inverted Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Hole‐transporting materials (HTMs) play an important role in transporting photogenerated holes, tuning the perovskite crystallization process, and passivating uncoordinated Pb2+ defects for high‐performance inverted perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, a unique cost‐effective small molecule‐type HTM based on a triarylamine core bearing a chloro‐assisted chelating moiety (named TPA‐CAA) is synthesized, which has excellent affinity to the perovskite precursor solution leading to smooth and uniform perovskite films. In comparison with the structurally similar molecule TPA‐AA with the absence of the chloro‐substituent, TPA‐CAA can form a chelate structure with Pb2+ via the carbonyl and the adjacent chloro‐atom, which efficiently tunes the perovskite crystallization, passivates the defects, and enhances the hole transporting at the perovskite/HTL interface. Eventually, the TPA‐CAA‐based inverted PSC achieves a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 21.56% (19.64% and 18.84% for the poly[bis(4‐phenyl)(2,4,6‐trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) and TPA‐AA ones, respectively) with a high open‐circuit voltage (VOC) of 1.113 V. Moreover, the stability of the TPA‐CAA‐based device is notably improved, and the PCE maintains over 80% of its initial value over 1000 h storage in ambient air (25 °C, relative humidity 30–40%) without encapsulation, in comparison to that of the PTAA device (only 50% of the initial value left over 1000 h).

Hole-transporting materials (HTMs) play an important role in transporting photogenerated holes, tuning the perovskite crystallization process, and passivating uncoordinated $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ defects for high-performance inverted perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, a unique cost-effective small molecule-type HTM based on a triarylamine core bearing a chloro-assisted chelating moiety (named TPA-CAA) is synthesized, which has excellent affinity to the perovskite precursor solution leading to smooth and uniform perovskite films. In comparison with the structurally similar molecule TPA-AA with the absence of the chloro-substituent, TPA-CAA can form a chelate structure with $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ via the carbonyl and the adjacent chloro-atom, which efficiently tunes the perovskite crystallization, passivates the defects, and enhances the hole transporting at the perovskite/HTL interface. Eventually, the TPA-CAA-based inverted PSC achieves a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $21.56\%$ ( $19.64\%$ and $18.84\%$ for the poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) and TPA-AA ones, respectively) with a high open-circuit voltage $(\pmb{V}_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of 1.113 V. Moreover, the stability of the TPA-CAA-based device is notably improved, and the PCE maintains over $80\%$ of its initial value over $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1000}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ storage in ambient air $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ relative humidity $30\text{\textperthousand}$ ) without encapsulation, in comparison to that of the PTAA device (only $50\%$ of the initial value left over 1000 h).
## 1. Introduction
Hybrid organic-inorganic halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been exploited significantly due to the excellent optoelectronic properties of perovskite materials including wide absorption band,[1–2] long carrier diffusion length,[3] and low exciton band energy.[4] To date, the state-of-the-art certificated power conversion efficiency (PCE) has reached up to $26.0\%$ , comparable to that of silicon solar cells.[5] Among the typical configurations of devices, although the efficiency of “inverted” $p$ -i-n type PSCs is slightly inferior to that of “regular” n-i$p$ devices, the former has the superiority in the aspect of low-temperature fabrication process, air and humidity stabilities,[6] and applicability for the tandem devices[7,8] along with the feasibility for the commercial roll-to-roll production systems.[9]
In the typical fabrication process for inverted PSCs, the perovskite absorber film is formed on the top of hole-transporting materials (HTMs), the chemical properties of which can thus significantly influence the crystal morphology and defect formation of perovskite.[10,11] Polymeric HTMs like poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6- trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA)[12,13] were ely investigated in p-i-n type inverted PSCs. However, due to   
the strong solvent resistance of the so-formed hole-transporting r (HTL) to the polar perovskite precursor inks contain  
ing N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF), and dimethyl sulfoxide SO), it is difficult for the precursor solutions to spread   
out uniformly on HTL. Thus, voids are easily formed during   
the device fabrication, which not only results in poor interfa  
cial contact between the perovskite layer and HTL but also in  
curs the degradation of perovskite crystals. Consequently, the   
photovoltaic performance under long-term operations would   
be significantly influenced.[14,15] To resolve these issues, two   
main strategies have been explored including 1) post-processing   
treatment by a surface modifier such as poly[(9,9-bis( $\left\lvert3^{\prime}\right\rvert$ -   
((N,N -dimethyl)-N -ethylammonium)-propyl)−2,7-fluorene)-alt  
2,7-(9,9-dioctylfluorene)] (PFN-Br)[16] and 2)the development of   
small molecule-type HTMs, so-called self-assembly materials
(SAMs)[17–18] which can form single molecular layer fixed on the indium tin oxide (ITO) glasses. Nevertheless, the addition of the PFN-Br would increase the fabrication cost and be always detrimental to device stability. For the latter, SAMs have essential requirements for anchoring groups like phosphoric acid[19] or carboxylic acid,[20] that restrict the structural design strategy of SAMs.
On the other hand, the popular PTAA cannot play a role in tuning the perovskite crystallization process or passivating the defects induced by the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , including Pb-related vacancy or antisite defects at the buried interface of perovskite due to the absence of other coordinating heteroatoms in these HTMs. These defects could result in the accumulation of photogenerated holes and thus serious electron-hole recombinations.[21] To tune the crystallization process of perovskite and suppress the $\mathrm{{Pb}}$ -related defect formation, single or more specific functional groups containing Lewis basic heteroatoms such as thiophene units,[22–24] carbonyls,[25,26] and cyano groups[27,28] were introduced into HTM molecules for use in inverted PSCs, which can form non-covalent interactions with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Thus, these heteroatoms would efficiently passivate the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ defects at the interface via the coordinating interaction between the Lewis base sites and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ .
Recently, the role of halide atoms such as Cl in tuning the perovskite crystallization or passivating the defects has attracted attention. Lin et al deposited Cl-terminated polar silanes, chloropropyltrimethoxysilane (CPS), as the passivation molecules on the surface of perovskite film, which suppressed the formation of defects because of the existing Cl atoms interacting with the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ .[29] In addition, the strong $\mathrm{\sfPb-Cl}$ bonding could passivate the deep-level trap state like $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ antisite defects. Sargent et al synthesized the Cl-capped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystal film via the reaction of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ with benzyl alcohol at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under ambient atmosphere. The antisite defects were suppressed at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /perovskite interface thanks to the high formation energy of Pb-Cl antisite. Simultaneously, the binding between Cl-capped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite has been enhanced due to the strong bonding of Cl atoms with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ .[30] To the best of our knowledge, most passivating molecules bearing Cl atoms are used as additives, but small molecule-type HTMs containing Cl substituents in inverted PSCs are limited. Furthermore, how the chloro-substituent in HTM molecules would influence the performance of inverted PSCs has not been systematically investigated.
Herein, for the first time we designed and synthesized a triarylamine-based small molecule-type HTM bearing a chlorosubstituent (denoted by TPA-CAA) for use in the inverted PSCs. It was demonstrated that the perovskite precursor solution could easily spread out uniformly on the TPA-CAA-based HTL. Moreover, it can enhance the binding of HTMs to perovskite film and induce the growth of high-quality crystals with fewer voids at the buried interfaces via the efficient chelating interactions between the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and the carbonyl moiety, as well as the Cl substituent present in HTM molecules. Encouragingly, the inverted devices with the structure of fluorine doped tin oxide $\mathrm{(FTO)/HTMs/CsFAMAPbI_{3\cdotx}B r_{x}/C_{60}/}$ bathocuproine $({\mathrm{BCP}})/{\mathrm{Ag}}$ where pristine TPA-CAA was used as HTMs exhibited a champion PCE of $21.56\%$ with a high open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of $1.113\mathrm{V}$ and fill factor (FF) of $82.95\%$ . The achieved PCE value is among the state-of-the-art cases for the inverted PSCs based on small molecule-type HTMs (Table S1, Supporting Information). Moreover, the air stability of the TPA-CAA-based device was improved significantly, so that the efficiency maintained over $80\%$ of its initial value after $1000\mathrm{h}$ storage in ambient air. In addition, it is notable that the synthetic cost of TPA-CAA is just $54~\mathfrak{S}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ much more cost-effective than conventional PTAA and other organic small molecule-type HTMs such as [2-(9|H|-Carbazol-9- yl)ethyl]phosphonic acid] (2PACz). The findings actually provide a valuable direction for the future molecular design of highly efficient and low-cost small molecule-type dopant-free HTMs for use in inverted PSCs.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Material Synthesis
The molecular structure and synthesis route for TPA-CCA are shown in Figure 1a and Scheme S1 (Supporting Information), respectively. The chemical structure and purity were identified by $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR and $^{13}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{NMR}$ spectroscopy together with matrixassisted laser desorption/ionization time-of-flight (MALDI-TOF) mass spectrometry. Due to the available cheap raw materials and facile synthesis route, the molecule can be obtained at a low cost $(54\mathrm{~\$~g~}^{-1})$ which is much cheaper than PTAA $(429\ \mathfrak{S}\ \mathfrak{g}^{-1})$ and 2PACz $(397\ \mathbb{S}\ \mathtt{g}^{-1})$ (calculation details see Scheme S2 and Table S2, Supporting Information).
## 2.2. Basic Properties of TPA-CAA
For inverted PSCs, under operation conditions, the sunlight will pass through the hole-transporting layer before reaching the perovskite layer. Therefore, the transmittance of HTM is critical. As shown in Figure 1b, there is a negligible difference between the transmittance spectra of the bare FTO glass and the one covered with TPA-CAA and PTAA within the range of wavelength greater than $500\mathrm{nm}$ . According to the UV–vis and fluorescence spectra (Figure S1, Supporting Information), the absorbance and emission wavelength of TPA-CAA are 338 and $410\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Consequently, the weak absorbance of TPA-CAA in the range of $320{-}380~\mathrm{nm}$ has a subtle influence on the transmittance of FTO glass. As shown in Figure 1c, the perovskite films with and without TPA-CAA showed almost the same UV–vis absorption spectrum, which indicated that the photo absorption of the perovskite layer was not influenced by the TPA-CAA HTL.
The thermal stability of HTMs is another basic characteristic to satisfy the requirement of working under high temperatures. Based on the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) shown in Figure S2a (Supporting Information), the decomposition temperature of TPA-CAA can be determined to be $391~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at $5\%$ weight loss, which is much higher than the annealing temperature of perovskite. The differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) analysis in Figure S2b (Supporting Information) demonstrated that, for TPA-CAA, no obvious endothermic peak appeared in the range of ambient temperature to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The results suggested that TPA-CAA would not undergo the glass transition so the normal annealing steps during the fabrication process and high-temperature working conditions should have no influence on the morphology of the underlying TPA-CAA film. Based on the above analysis, it is believed that TPA-CAA has the potential for use as a suitable HTM in inverted PSCs.
Due to the better affinity of TPA-CAA to organic solvents, the contact angle for the precursor solution (mixed solvent of DMF and DMSO in a volume ratio of 4:1) on the surface of TPA-CAA film was found to be only $11^{\circ}$ , much smaller than that on the PTAA surface $(27^{\circ})$ , as shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information). Thus, it is expected to be beneficial for the precursor solution to uniformly spread out on the TPA-CAA HTL surface to realize the smooth growth of perovskite crystals. Since TPA-CAA could be dissolved in DMF or DMSO, the presence of TPA-CAA after rinsing the HTL with the precursor solvent was confirmed by MALDI-TOF and Energy Dispersive Spectrometer (EDS), as shown in Figures S4 and S5 (Supporting Information), respectively. In Figure S4 (Supporting Information), TPA-CAA (the signal at $\mathrm{m/z}=548.1_{\cdot}$ ) still existed in the chloroform solutions obtained by soaking the TPA-CAA films (after rinsing by the precursor solvent with $\mathrm{V_{DMF}:V_{D M S O}=4:1)}$ into chloroform. As shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information), the green dots indicate the Cl element, which is uniformly distributed on the surface washed by the precursor solvent.
The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy levels were obtained by the cyclic voltammetry (CV) method with ferrocene as an internal standard. The CV curves of TPA-CAA are shown in Figure S6a,b (Supporting Information), and the HOMO of TPA-CAA was calculated to be $-5.09\mathrm{eV}.$ The energy level of perovskite films was characterized via ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS, Figure S6c, Supporting Information), and the valence band level of perovskite was evaluated to be $5.90\ \mathrm{eV}$ by Equation (1).
$$
E_{\mathrm{{VB}}}=21.22~e V-E_{\mathrm{{cutoff}}}+E_{\mathrm{{initial}}}
$$
where the $E_{\mathrm{cuttoff}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{initial}}$ represent the cutoff energy and initial energy of perovskite films. Moreover, according to the light absorption spectra (Figure 1c; Figure S1, Supporting Information), the bandgaps $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of TPA-CAA and perovskite were determined to be 3.36 and $1.{\bar{5}}8\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, by Tauc plots (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The energy level alignment of different layers is summarized in Figure S8 (Supporting Information), suggesting that TPA-CAA is capable of extracting holes to cathode and block electrons functioning as HTMs.
The hole mobility $(\mu_{\mathrm{h}})$ values of TPA-CAA and PTAA were determined by the space charge limited current (SCLC) method based on hole-only devices of FTO/PEDOT: PSS/HTMs/Ag in darkness. The corresponding $J{-}V$ curves are shown in Figure S9 (Supporting Information). The $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ values of TPA-CAA and PTAA were found to be $9.21\times10^{-4}$ and $7.03\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , respectively. It is supposed that, for TPA-CAA, the enhancement of $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ benefits from the excellent $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking property of the triarylamine core unit that provides plenty of hole transporting channel.
To evaluate the chemical interaction between perovskite films and TPA-CAA, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements of the perovskite film and the one coated on TPA-CAA were performed. As shown in Figure 1d, for the bare perovskite film, there were two main peaks of $\mathrm{Pb~}4\mathrm{f}~7/2$ and $\mathrm{\sfPb}$ 4f 5/2 at 138.47 and $143.32\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Meanwhile, two weak peaks related to ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ were observed at the lower binding energy (BE) of 137.11 and $142.19\mathrm{eV.}$ However, the peaks of ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ disappeared and the two Pb 4f signals shifted to lower BEs at 138.35 and $143.22\mathrm{eV}$ for the perovskite covered by TPA-CAA,99 which suggested that TPA-CAA interacted with the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ on the surface of the perovskite film. To further confirm the origin of these interactions, the XPS spectra of O 1s and Cl 2p were obtained, as shown in Figure 1e,f. According to the peak-fitting spectrum of O 1s, compared to the pristine TPA-CAA, the peak of C─O bonding for TPA-CAA covered by the perovskite film was unchanged while that of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bonding shifted to lower bonding energy from 532.65 to $532.44\mathrm{eV.}$ In addition, a new peak attributed to Pb─O $(532.86\mathrm{eV})$ could be found, which cannot be observed for the bare perovskite film.[31] These results indicated that the lone pair electrons on the oxygen atom from the carbonyl group of TPA-CAA coordinated with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions in the perovskite film. Interestingly, the peaks of Cl 2p 1/2 at $200.78\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}2\mathrm{p}3/2$ at $202.31\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the pristine TPA-CAA shifted to higher binding energy levels at 200.91 and $202.51\ \mathrm{eV};$ respectively, for the TPA-CAA film covered by perovskite, as shown in the $\operatorname{Cl}2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum. It is inferred that the Cl atom in TPA-CAA interacted with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions in the perovskite, leading to the shifting of the corresponding binding energies. Thus, the carbonyl and the adjacent Cl substituent might form a chelate with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , which played an important role in passivating uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ defects and tuning the growth of perovskite crystals above.
## 2.3. The Influences of Different HTMs on Perovskite Crystal Morphology
To further evaluate the role of the Cl atom in the interaction between TPA-CAA and perovskite, we designed and synthesized a similar molecule with the absence of the chloro-substituent, named TPA-AA with the structure shown in Figure 2a. To obtain the optimum geometry of TPA-CAA and TPA-AA and further investigate their electronic structures, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed by the Gaussian $16~\mathrm{\textW}$ program at the B3LYP level. Figure 2b,c illustrates the optimized structure and electrostatic potential maps of TPA-CAA and TPAAA, respectively. Commonly, red areas stand for negative potential regions that tend to donate electrons to a metal ion, such as uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions at the grain boundaries (GBs) and the surface of the perovskite film. Obviously, for both molecules, the O atom of the carbonyl group had the largest electron densities (red), which could efficiently interact with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ as Lewis basic sites and thus tune the crystallization of perovskite. Notably, even though in TPA-CAA, the Cl atom exhibited slightly weaker negative potentials, the Cl atom close to the carbonyl oxygen in TPACAA has a potential to combine with the O atom leading to the formation of a chelate structure with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , as shown in Figure 2d. The chelation was expected to efficiently passivate the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions and enhance the crystallinity of perovskite films, and the schematic illustration of the chelation of TPA-CAA with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions at the buried perovskite films is shown in Figure 2e.
The $\mathrm{^1H}$ -NMR was carried out to verify the interaction difference between two HTMs (TPA-AA and TPA-CAA) and perovskite. The NMR spectra of TPA-CAA and TPA-AA dissolved in DMSO- $.d6$ in the absence and presence of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ are obtained in Figure S10 (Supporting Information). An obvious high-field chemical shift of $\Delta\delta=0.029$ ppm (from 4.241 to $4.212\mathrm{ppm}_{,}$ for protons in the methylene group of TPA-AA could be observed, while the signal for the methyl of TPA-AA shifted only from 2.036 to $2.021\mathrm{ppm}$ $\Delta\delta=0.015\mathrm{ppm})$ . It indicated that more electrons donated from TPA-CAA to perovskite than from TPA-AA, probably because not only the O atom but also the Cl atom in the chloroacetamide chain of TPA-CAA would interact with the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . This is another evidence of the chelate structure formation between the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ion and chloroacetamide, which could tune the formation of perovskite crystal seeds in spin coating and further crystallization in the annealing process.
To investigate the influence of HTMs on the morphology of perovskite films and the crystal qualities, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements were carried out for the perovskite films prepared on different HTM layers. In the top-view SEM images of the perovskite film surface (Figure S11, Supporting Information), perovskite grains on the TPA-CAA film with an average size of $250~\mathrm{nm}$ were comparable to those on TPAAA film $(240\mathrm{nm})$ and larger than those on PTAA film ( $(180\mathrm{nm})$ . Figure $2\mathrm{f-h}$ shows the cross-section view of the perovskite film on different HTMs where the difference in grain size was more pronounced, probably because the perovskite crystallization occurs from top to bottom in the solution coating process[14] and perovskite at the up-surface has insufficient interaction with HTMs at the buried interface. As shown, for the TPA-CAA-based perovskite sample, near the buried interface of perovskite, the perovskite film consisted of fewer and larger crystals between 372 and $548\mathrm{nm}$ , while perovskite films in TPA-AA- and PTAA-based devices exhibited multiple crystals with grain size from 231 to $386\mathrm{nm}$ and from 174 to $342\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, which should form a superior morphology with fewer GBs and enhance photogenerated excitons transporting in the bulk TPA-CAA-based perovskite layers.[32] Moreover, perovskite films with TPA-CAA and PTAA showed a dense and compact stack with FTO/HTMs substrate, while there were more voids at the buried interface for TPA-AA, which could lead to inferior interfacial charge transport. To further examine the surface morphologies, atomic force microscope (AFM) measurements were performed on the perovskite films deposited on different HTMs. As shown in Figure S12 (Supporting Information), the root-mean-square (RMS) roughness values were 10.1, 14.9, and $15.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the perovskite films grown on TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA, respectively. A smooth and flat perovskite film would facilitate the contact between the photoabsorption layer and the electron-transporting layer, and could thus reduce the loss of $\dot{\boldsymbol{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ , which was consistent with the results of SEM measurement for grain size.
To further explore the influence of TPA-CAA and TPA-AA on the crystallinity of perovskite films, X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were performed on the corresponding perovskite films. The XRD patterns are shown in Figure 2i,j. It was found that, for the TPA-CAA-based perovskite film, the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of the dominant diffraction peak at $14.4^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (100) crystal plane was sharper, and the intensity ratios of (100) to (110) facet and (200) to (210) facet were both enhanced compared to those for samples based on TPA-AA and PTAA. The results indicated that TPA-CAA was more favorable to promote the growth of the orientation (h00) facet owing to the stronger interaction with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , compared to TPA-AA and PTAA, leading to much greater crystallinity for perovskite films. Interestingly, it was found that for the sample based on TPA-CAA, the intensity ratio of (100) to (111) is smaller than those based on other HTMs. It is evidenced that the (100) facet is more susceptible to undergo corrosion from moisture than the (111) facet.[33] Thus, the device involving TPA-CAA was expected to have superior stability than that based on PTAA and TPA-AA. Overall, based on the above analysis, TPA-CAA should be able to efficiently tune the crystallization process of perovskite, reduce defects at GBs and the perovskite/HTM interface, optimize carrier transport, and enhance the device stabilities.
To further evaluate the perovskite quality on different HTMs, the hole trap density $\left(n_{\mathrm{t}}\right)$ by the SCLC method based on hole-only devices of FTO/HTMs (TPA-CAA/TPAAA/PTAA)/perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Ag in darkness was obtained. The corresponding current-voltage curves are exhibited in Figure S13 (Supporting Information). The transition voltage from the ohmic region to the trap-filling limit region is referred to as $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ . $n_{\mathrm{t}}$ can be calculated by the equation: $n_{\mathrm{t}}=2\epsilon\epsilon_{0}V_{\mathrm{TFL}}/e L^{2}$ , where $\epsilon$ , $\epsilon_{0}$ , e, and $L$ represent dielectric constant, vacuum permittivity, element charge, and thickness of perovskite films, respectively. The perovskite film based on TPA-CAA exhibited a $n_{\mathrm{t}}$ of $1.02\times10^{14}~\mathrm{{cm}^{-3}}$ , while those based on TPA-AA and PTAA showed a higher $n_{\mathrm{t}}$ of $1.53\times10^{14}$ and $1.34\times10^{14}\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. The results indicated that the incorporation of TPA-CAA as HTMs suppressed the hole trap density at the perovskite/HTL interface and the nonradiative recombination loss due to the improved morphology of perovskite film grown on TPA-CAA films.
## 2.4. The Influences of Different HTMs on Interfacial Charge Transport
To investigate the charge transfer at the interface between HTM and perovskite, we utilized Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) to obtain the contact potential difference (CPD) of perovskite films deposited on TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA (Figure S14, Supporting Information) along with bare HTL based on FTO (Figure S15, Supporting Information). According to the CPD linear spectra of perovskite films shown in Figure 3a, the average CPDs of perovskite on TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA were found to be 250, 305, and $204~\mathrm{mV},$ respectively. The work function $(E_{\mathrm{f}})$ of thin films can be calculated according to Equations (2–4).[34]
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{CPD}=\mathrm{\Lambda}({\mathbb{W}}_{\mathrm{tip}}-{\mathbb{W}}_{\mathrm{f}})/e}\\ &{}\\ &{{\mathbb{W}}_{\mathrm{f}}=5.1\mathrm{eV}+e\left(\mathrm{CPD}_{\mathrm{Au}}-\mathrm{CPD}_{\mathrm{sample}}\right)}\\ &{{\mathbb{E}}_{\mathrm{f}}=-{\mathbb{W}}_{\mathrm{f}}}\end{array}
$$
$\mathsf{W}_{\mathrm{tip}}$ and $\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{f}}$ are the work functions of the probe tip and the sample surface, respectively, $e$ is the charge of the electron, and $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ is the Fermi Level of perovskite. Normally, the $\mathsf{W}_{\mathrm{tip}}$ value of the tip was calibrated by the Au sample with $W_{\mathrm{Au}}=5.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{CPD}_{\mathrm{Au}}=-60\mathrm{mV}.$ Thus, the Fermi Levels $(E_{\mathrm{f}})$ of perovskite based on TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA were calculated to be $-4.79$ , $-4.74$ , and $-4.84\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Similarly, the $\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{f}}$ of TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA coated on FTO glasses were measured to be 5.05, 5.08, and $5.00\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. It is suggested that the TPA-CAA- and TPA-AA-based perovskite films tended to exhibit n-type semiconductor characteristics compared to those based on PTAA. Furthermore, it has been reported that the perovskite with reduced $E_{f}$ is more efficient in electron extraction and transporting at the perovskite/ $C_{60}$ interface, resulting in less interface charge recombination.[35,36] Moreover, as shown in Figure 3d, the PSC involving TPA-CAA exhibited the lowest leakage current which is related to interfacial charge transport, followed by devices based on PTAA and TPA-AA, which indicated the optimization of the charge extraction and the suppression of defects for TPA-CAA-based devices.[24] Therefore, a higher $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ value should be obtained for the PSC based on TPA-CAA, compared to that for the PTAA- and TPA-AA-based devices, which agreed with the photovoltaic results obtained for these cells. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted at an applied voltage of $0.6\mathrm{V}$ and the Nyquist plots fitted with the equivalent circuit diagram in the inset are shown in Figure 3c. The charge recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ was determined to be $1773\Omega$ for the TPACAA-based devices, which was much larger than PTAA-based ones $(1155~\Omega)$ and TPA-AA-based ones $(874\Omega)$ . At low applied bias conditions, the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is determined by the charge transport of the perovskite film.[37] The device based on TPA-CAA exhibited a better contact at the HTL/perovskite interface which results in a superior $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Furthermore, the interfacial contact in the TPAAA-based device was inferior to PTAA which corresponds to the results of SEM measurement.
To further investigate the photo-induced charge separation and extraction process that occurred in perovskite films above different HTMs, steady-state photoluminescence (SSPL) and timeresolved photoluminescence (TRPL) were conducted, as shown in Figure 3d,e. Obviously, compared with that of the bare perovskite film, the emission intensities of perovskite films coated on TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA were quenched by $23.9\%$ $64.4\%$ , and $44.7\%$ , respectively, suggesting that TPA-CAA was the most efficient in inducing the photogenerated hole-electron pair separation in perovskite layer and collecting the holes. TRPL curves were fitted by a biexponential decay formula: $f(t)=A_{1}$ $e x p(-t/\tau_{1})+A_{2}~e x p(-t/\tau_{2})+B$ , where $\tau_{1}$ represents the fastdecay time constant, $\tau_{2}$ stands for the slow-decay time constant, and $A$ is the corresponding decay amplitude along with a constant B. The corresponding fitting parameters are summarized in Table S3 (Supporting Information). It is known that $\tau_{1}$ can be attributed to the $\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{f}}$ of FTO glass covered by HTMs and the passivation of interfacial defects between perovskites and HTL. Moreover, $\tau_{2}$ can be attributed to either the passivation of defects at the interface between HTMs and perovskites or the crystallinity of the perovskite films above HTL.[38] The lifetimes $\tau_{1}$ of TPA-CAA, TPA-AA, and PTAA were found to be 0.85, 0.93, and 1.02 ns, respectively, while the lifetimes $\tau_{1}$ were 24.1, 23.04, and 16.67 ns. According to the measurements above, on the one hand, owing to the moderate $\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{f}}$ , and the best ability to passivate the interfacial defects which can tune the crystal growth for enhanced perovskite morphology, TPA-CAA-based perovskite film showed the shortest $\tau_{1}$ and the longest $\tau_{2}$ . On the other hand, for TPA-AA, the corresponding perovskite has a better crystallinity than that for PTAA, but the $\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{f}}$ was high-lying and the perovskite/HTMs interfacial contact was inferior. Therefore, perovskite on TPA-AA was calculated to have a moderate $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ .
## 2.5. Photovoltaic Performance of TPA-CAA-Based Devices
In this study, the p-i-n device with a configuration of FTO/HTM $\mathrm{5/CS_{0.05}(F A_{0.92}M A_{0.08})_{0.95}P b(I_{0.92}B r_{0.08})_{3}/C_{60}/B C P/A g}$ was adopted and the PTAA-based device fabricated as the control without any dopants in HTMs (shown as Figure 4a). The fabrication process of PSCs was depicted in supporting information. The concentration of TPA-CAA was optimized to be $6.0\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ (in chloroform) to obtain the best performance (Figure S16, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 4b and Table 1, the PCE of the champion device reached up to $21.56\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.113 V, $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ of $23.33\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and FF of $82.95\%$ for the TPA-CAA-based PSC under optimal conditions, which is far greater than that of the control device $(19.64\%)$ with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.037\mathrm{V},J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.81~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $82.98\%$ . Interestingly, both $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values of the TPA-CAA-based PSC significantly improved with a lower hysteresis index (HI), compared to those of the PTAA one, probably due to the greatly enhanced perovskite crystal quality and interfacial contact.[32] In addition, the value of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ is calculated by external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum (Figure 4c) for the TPA-CAA and PTAA-based devices (22.89 and $22.32\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively) to verify the reliability of those achieved from the $J{-}V$ curve (within the deviation of $5\%$ ). The photoelectrical paraments of PSCs based on two HTMs were presented in Figure 4d–f, which suggested that the improved PCE of the TPA-CAA-based device could result from the enhancement of all the photovoltaic parameters, compared to that of the PTAA device. In a comparison with the results reported in the literature (Table S1, supporting information), the PCE of the TPA-CAA-based device in this work reached an above-average performance among the inverted PSCs based on dopant-free small molecule HTMs. Meanwhile, the PSCs based on TPA-AA were fabricated and the photovoltaic characteristics and $J{-}V$ curve of the champion device were obtained, as shown in Figures S17 and S18 (Supporting Information). The champion PCE of $18.84\%$ was achieved with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.16~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.076\mathrm{V},$ and FF of $79.0\%$ . Compared to those obtained for the TPA-CAA- and PTAA-based PSC, the worst performance may be attributed to the insufficient defect passivation and inferior interfacial contact between perovskite and HTL. The results indicated that the introduction of the Cland O-based chelating moiety could be a promising strategy for the design of efficient small molecule-type HTMs for use in inverted PSCs.
## 2.6. The stability of the TPA-CAA-based devices
The long-term stability of PSCs is critical for their practical applications. Thus, we further evaluated the device stabilities of PSCs based on TPA-CAA and PTAA at room temperature in argon atmosphere (Figure 5a) and ambient air (Figure 5b). In an argon environment, TPA-CAA-based devices could achieve $92\%$ of the original PCE value after $1000\mathrm{h}$ storage, while PTAA- and TPA-AA-based cells only remained at $88\%$ and $81\%$ of the initial PCE, respectively. Encouragingly, under ambient air $(25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ relative humidity $30\mathrm{-}40\%)$ , the PCE of the TPA-CAA-based devices maintained over $80\%$ of the initial performance after storage for ${\approx}1000\ \mathrm{h}$ (only $50\%$ and $47\%$ left for the PTAA device and TPA-AA device, respectively), which is corresponding to the results of XRD patterns. As shown in Figure 5c, in contrast to the control, TPA-CAA-based devices have higher photocurrent and lower current decreases under continuous operating conditions at maximum power point (MPP) for $400\mathrm{s}$ All these results reflected that the better crystallinity of perovskite and the passivation effect on perovskite interface defects involving the TPA-CAA HTM can not only improve the device performance, but also significantly enhance the stability of the device.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we designed and synthesized a unique small molecule-type HTM (denoted by TPA-CAA) based on the simple triarylamine group bearing a chloro-assisted chelating moiety with the low synthetic cost $(\approx54\ \S\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ for use in inverted PSCs without the addition of dopants. Compared to PTAA, TPACAA had a better affinity to the perovskite precursor solution and thus the precursor solution could more uniformly spread out on the TPA-CAA HTL surface to realize smooth growth of perovskite crystals. In comparison with the structurally similar molecule TPA-AA with the absence of the chloro-substituent, TPA-CAA could form a chelate structure with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ via the carbonyl and the adjacent chloro-atom. The detailed comparisons of the XRD patterns and SEM images for perovskite films based on TPA-CAA and TPA-AA demonstrated that the chelating interaction between TPA-CAA and perovskite resulted in the perovskite crystal with greater crystallinity and fewer voids, and better perovskite/HTL interfacial contact. Thus, the hole transport was enhanced at the buried interface of the perovskite film coated on the TPA-CAA HTL, and a higher $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of inverted PSCs was obtained. The inverted planar PSCs using TPA-CAA as HTMs obtained a champion PCE of $21.56\%$ with a high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ value of $1.113\mathrm{V}$ and FF of $82.98\%$ , reaching an above-average performance among the inverted PSCs based on dopant-free small molecule HTMs. Moreover, the stability of PSCs based on TPA-CAA is improved remarkably, which maintained $92\%$ of their initial PCE over $1000\mathrm{h}$ storage in ambient air $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ relative humidity $30\text{\textperthousand}$ , without encapsulation, compared to that based on PTAA (only $50\%$ of the initial value left over $1000\mathrm{h}$ ). This work demonstrated the feasibility of the chloro-assisted chelating HTMs that could passivate the defects and tune the crystallization for perovskite film via the chelation with uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , which provides a new design strategy for the future development of small molecule HTMs applied in the efficient and stable inverted PSCs.
